  {
   "user": "bistromathtician",
   "message": "There's way too many degrees of freedom in the examples he's pulling from for there to be any \"real\" mythos driving this particular saga (suicide actually seems pretty rare in these high-profile frauds so I don't think it can be real archetype).  But Baruch's spin on the MN is probably pointing to something real in the sense that the theodicy of Woke 1 requires people who can find the structural oppression in any situation, and Arday was perfect at this for this because he could simply make up whatever details would serve his purpose.  I suspect his entire field having terrible epistemics made it far easier for him to get away with his fabulism, since digging too hard into any particular research would quickly uncover the lack of rigor/truth in the vast majority of education studies.\nI thought Tyler Austin Harper's recent article https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/2026/09/elite-academia-leadership-hypocrisy/687969/ was a much better description of what academia is looking for (and looking away from) from someone who is deliberately choosing not to be like Arday.",
   "timestamp": "2026-08-23T21:46:44.558000+00:00",
